  "novel_hypothesis_tested": "L6 inverts the entire helper-robot premise: the color-15 robot is a THIEF, not a helper. The west 9-border bay STARTS with a block correctly placed at slot (4,7); the thief spawns at (4,4), autonomously EXTRACTS the bay block, hauls it east through a double-wide wall via a tunnel, and dumps it on decoy pad tiles, then parks. The decoy pads (three unbordered color-2 tiles east of the wall, where bay-interior color-2 normally means destination) are a trap: filling them in any 2-block configuration does nothing. The win is to DISMISS the thief -- a brand-new verb: stand adjacent facing it (it contact-highlights with a color-11 outline like blocks do) and press ACTION5, which removes it from the level permanently -- then hand-ferry BOTH blocks (the stolen one and the pre-existing east block) back through the row-6 wall tunnel (player-passable; the row-5 opening is not) into the west bay: restore (4,7) and fill (4,8). levels_completed advanced to 6 exactly on the second bay placement.",
  "narrative": "Replayed the 359-action prefix (re-confirming the L5 candidate tail en route). Episode 1 (contaminated entry): mapped the level blind -- watched a block teleport across the wall, found the row-6 tunnel is player-passable, cornered the parked robot in the tunnel, discovered bump-highlight then ACTION5 REMOVES it, retrieved the dumped block, and empirically refuted the east-pad-as-destination model by trying two fill configurations, then placed a block in the west bay just before expiry. Episode 2 (pristine entry after reset): hands-off observation revealed the true opening -- the bay starts with a correctly-placed block that the THIEF steals and dumps east, then idles; confirmed no respawn/refill cycle. Episode 3: executed the 56-action winning script -- chase the thief to its parking tile, dismiss it with ACTION5, carry the stolen block back through the tunnel to its exact original slot (4,7), fetch the east block (one mid-tunnel ghost re-side required), and place it at (4,8); the level completed on that drop, live-observed. Bonus recon: L7 is a compressed single-corridor thief level (tall two-row bay, two blocks, one thief, decoy pads, massive walls above and below).",
  "l6_gotchas": [
    "The color-15 robot is a THIEF: it steals the pre-placed bay block at level start and dumps it on east-side decoy pads. Do not model it as a helper.",
    "NEW VERB -- robot dismissal: stand adjacent facing the robot (it contact-highlights with a color-11 outline) and press ACTION5; it is removed from the level permanently. No other interaction affects it.",
    "The row-6 wall column is a player-passable tunnel; the row-5 opening next to it is not player-passable. Detect passability empirically per tile, not from wall texture.",
    "The east color-2 pad tiles are DECOYS: bay-interior color-2 normally marks destinations, but unbordered pads here accept blocks without ever advancing the level.",
    "The win required restoring the stolen block to its EXACT original slot (4,7) plus the second block in an adjacent slot (4,8); (4,8)+(3,8)-style pairs were not tested, but (4,8) alone and all east-pad configs were refuted.",
    "The prefix bleeds 5 leftover tail pumps into L6, consuming 6 timer cells and advancing the thief phase -- candidate scripts must be phase-robust or start from a timer-forced reset.",
    "L6 timer drain ~0.83 cells/action (~77 actions/episode) -- tighter than L5.",
    "frobots/color-15 detectors miss the robot in its contact-highlighted form (outline turns color 11); scan both colors."
  ],
